<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-2-2-boolean-expressions">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Boolean Expressions</title>
   <idx>boolean</idx>
    <idx>boolean expression</idx>
    

  <introduction>
    <p>Apps are a lot more fun if they can respond to different user actions and decisions. For example, if you are playing a game, the app can check whether 
      you have enough points to move to the next level. A social media app needs to ask you for your password and check that
      it is correct before allowing you to post a comment. Apps use <term>Boolean expressions</term> to make decisions based on true or false 
      conditions, like in the flowchart below.
      In this lesson, you will learn how to use Boolean expressions in your code to change an app's behavior.
    </p>
    <image source="_static/Unit2Images/password-flowchart.png" width="70%" alt="Is your password correct flowchart"/>
  </introduction>

 
  <subsection xml:id="decisions">
    <title>Boolean Expressions and Decision Making</title>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Apps often check conditions that have only two outcomes, such as whether a user is logged 
        in, whether a checkbox is selected, or whether a password is correct.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Programs make decisions by evaluating conditions that result in a Boolean value. 
        A <term>Boolean value</term> has two possible values: <term>true</term> or <term>false</term>.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>In Python, <term>Boolean values</term> are represented by the keywords <c>True</c> and <c>False</c>.</p>

      <activity label="boolvalues0">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try to guess what the code below will print out before you run it.
          Change didHomework to True and run again.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
didHomework = False
cleanedRoom = True
print("I did everything?", cleanedRoom and didHomework )
      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("True",self.getOutput(),"Change didHomework to True and run again.")

          myTests().main()
      </tests>
       
      </program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Apps can use Boolean expressions to decide what happens next, such as showing a message, 
        allowing access, or changing what a user sees. A <term>Boolean expression</term> is an expression that 
        evaluates to either true or false.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Many Boolean expressions use comparisons, such as checking whether a score is high enough or 
        whether an input meets a requirement like password length.
      </p>
    </note>

 <p>Let's think about the pizza web app again. It lets the user choose different options, and
    then chooses different outputs based on the user's choices.  </p>

    <interactive iframe="webapps/pizzaApp.html" width="100%" aspect="2.5:1.2" />

    <activity label="pizzaAppBools">
      <statement>
        <p>Interact with the pizza app above.
          Which of the following are Boolean expressions (True or False) that the app 
          uses to determine its output? 
          Select all that apply.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <choices  randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>User likes pizza</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Yes, the app needs to check whether the user likes pizza to display it and pizza images in
              the output.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>User's name is 10 characters long</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app doesn't care how long the user's name is.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>User's age is not less than 0</statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This app doesn't care what the user types in for their age. 
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>There is a favorite color</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Yes, the app uses the favorite color value to change the background to that color.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="relational-operators">
    <title>Relational Operators</title>

    <idx>relational operator</idx>
    <idx>&lt;</idx>
    <idx>&gt;</idx>
    <idx>==</idx>
    <idx>!=</idx>


    
    <note>
      <title>2.2.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Apps and programs often compare values to enforce rules, personalize experiences, and 
        prevent mistakes. <term>Relational operators</term> allow programs to evaluate questions such as whether a value is too low or whether one value matches another.
      </p>
    </note>

      <p><term>Relational operators</term> are operators like less than (<c>&lt;</c>), greater than (<c>&gt;</c>), and equal to (<c>==</c>) that compare two values or expressions. They are often used 
      to compare a variable against a constant value or another variable or an expression.
      For example, the following <term>Boolean expressions</term> (which evaluate to true or false)
      can be used to see whether a number is positive or negative by seeing if it is greater than 0 or less than 0:
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
# Test if a number is positive (greater than 0)
number &gt; 0
# Test if a number is negative (less than 0)
number &lt; 0
      </code>
    </program>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.B.2</title>
      <p>
        <term>Relational operators</term> compare two values and produce a Boolean result.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><term>Equals (==):</term> The expression <c>a == b</c> evaluates to true when the values of a and b are equal, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
        <li><term>Greater than (&gt;):</term> The expression <c>a &gt; b</c> evaluates to true when the value of a is greater than the value of b, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
        <li><term>Less than (&lt;):</term> The expression <c>a &lt; b</c> evaluates to true when the value of a is less than the value of b, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
        <li><term>Greater than or equal to (&gt;=):</term> The expression <c>a &gt;= b</c> evaluates to true when the value of a is greater than or equal to the value of b, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
        <li><term>Less than or equal to (&lt;=):</term> The expression <c>a &lt;= b</c> evaluates to true when the value of a is less than or equal to the value of b, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
      </ul>
    </note>

    <p>
      The relational operators <c>==</c> and <c>!=</c> (not equal) can be used
      to compare values for equality. They return True or False boolean values. 
      In Python, they can be used to compare numbers, strings, and other data types. For string comparisons like <c>"hello" != "Hello"</c>, 
      Python will check that each character is the same (including its case) in the two strings. 
      Don't confuse <c>=</c> and <c>==</c>. 
      One <c>=</c> sign changes the value of a variable. Two <c>==</c> equal
      signs are used to test if a variable holds a certain value, without
      changing its value!
  </p>

    <p>
      The relational operators <c>&lt;</c> and <c>&gt;</c> can be used to compare values and determine whether one is less than or greater than the other. 
      If you have trouble telling <c>&lt;</c> and <c>&gt;</c> apart, remember that 
      less than sign (<c>&lt;</c>) looks more like an L and always starts with the small pointy-end. To remember the correct order of the two characters in <c>&lt;=</c> and
      <c>&gt;=</c>, just write them in the same order you would say them in
      English: “less than or equal to” not “equal to or less than”.
    </p>

    <activity label="BooleanExps">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Drag the boolean expression from the left and drop it on what it is
          testing on the right. Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are
          correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Review the relational operators above.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise order="1">x &gt; 0</premise>
          <response>x is positive</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="2">x == y</premise>
          <response>x equals y</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="3">x &lt; 0</premise>
          <response>x is negative</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="4">x != y</premise>
          <response>x does not equal y</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="5">x &lt; y</premise>
          <response>x is less than y</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="6">x &gt; y</premise>
          <response>x is greater than y</response>
        </match>

        <match>
          <premise order="7">x &gt;= y</premise>
          <response>x is greater than or equal to y</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>


   <note>
      <title>2.2.B.3</title>
      <p>
        The result of a relational expression can be determined by substituting the current values, 
        including variable values, and evaluating whether the comparison is true or false.
      </p>
  </note>
 

  <activity label="boolvalues1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try to guess what the code below will print out before you run it.
          Note that 1 equal sign (<c>=</c>) is used for assigning a value and 2
          equal signs (<c>==</c>) for testing values. Then, add 3 more lines of
          code that sets cash to 15 and prints out whether cost is equal to cash
          and whether cost is not equal to cash.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
word1 = "hello"
word2 = "Hello"
# Will these print true or false?
print(word1 == word2)

cost = 15
cash = 12
# Will these print true or false?
print(cost == cash)
# Will these print true or false?
print(cost != cash)

# 1. Set cash to 15

# 2. Print whether cost is equal to cash

# 3. Print whether cost is not equal to cash

      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("False\nFalse\nTrue\nTrue\nFalse",self.getOutput(),"Output")

          myTests().main()
      </tests>
       
      </program>
    </activity>

  <activity label="boolvalues2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try to guess what the code below will print out before you run it.
          Then, set cash to 15 and print the boolean expression for whether cost
          is less than or equal to cash.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
cost = 15
cash = 12
# Will these print true or false?
print(cost &lt; cash)
# Will these print true or false?
print(cost &gt; cash)
# 1. Set cash to 15

# 2. Print whether cost is less than or equal to cash

      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("False\nTrue\nTrue\n",self.getOutput(),"Output")
              self.assertIn("&lt;=",self.getEditorText(), "Use &lt;=")

          myTests().main()
      </tests>
       
      </program>
    </activity>
  
    <activity label="trace-bool-expr">
        <statement>
            <p>What is printed after the following code executes?
            </p>
          <program language="python">
            <code>
            apples = 5
            bananas = 2
            oranges = 3
            print(apples &gt;= oranges + bananas)
            </code>
          </program>
        </statement>
        <choices  randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>False</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>No, apples is 5 and oranges + bananas is 5, so they are greater than or equal to each other.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>True</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! apples is 5 and oranges + bananas is 5, so they are greater than or equal to each other.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>5</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>No, the expression evaluates to a Boolean value, True or False.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>=</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>No, the expression evaluates to a Boolean value, True or False.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
      </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="logical-operators">
    <title>Logical Operators</title>
     <idx>logical operator</idx>
     <idx>and</idx>
     <idx>or</idx>
     <idx>not</idx>
     
    <p><term>Logical operators</term> like <c>and</c>, <c>or</c>, and <c>not</c> are used to combine Boolean expressions.
      For example, the following <term>Boolean expression</term> (which evaluates to true or false) can be used to see whether a 
      number is between 1 and 10:
      </p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
# Test if a number is between 1 and 10
number &gt; 1 and number &lt; 10
        </code>
      </program>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Apps and programs often combine multiple conditions to make decisions that feel predictable 
        and personalized. <term>Logical operators</term> allow programs to answer questions such as whether all 
        conditions are true, whether at least one condition is true, or whether a condition is not 
        true. Logical operators can also support user-centered rules, such as allowing an action 
        only when multiple conditions are met, like when all required fields are complete and the 
        user agrees to terms.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.C.2</title>
      <p>
        Logical operators combine or modify Boolean values and produce a Boolean result.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The expression <c>condition1 and condition2</c> evaluates to true when both conditions 
          are true, and evaluates to false otherwise.</li>
        <li>The expression <c>condition1 or condition2</c> evaluates to true when at least one 
          condition is true, and evaluates to false when both conditions are false.</li>
        <li>The expression <c>not condition</c> evaluates to true when the condition is false, and 
          evaluates to false when the condition is true.</li>
      </ul>
    </note>

    <p>The operator <c>and</c> is used to combine two things and verify that BOTH are true. The operator <c>or</c> is used to 
      verify that at least one is true. The operator <c>not</c> is used to see if something is not true. 
      With numerical values, the <term>and</term> operator is often used to see if a number is
      in an range, for example to get a B grade, your grade must be between 80 <c>and</c> 90 (not including 90). 
    </p>

<activity label="logical-operators">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try to guess what the code below will print out before you run it. Then, 
          test for one more grade range using a logical operator.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
grade = 80
# Will these print true or false?
print("Did you get an A?", grade &gt;= 90 and grade &lt; 100  )
print("Did you get an B?", grade &gt;= 80 and grade &lt; 90  )
print("Did you get an A or a B?", grade &gt;= 90 or grade &gt;= 80  )
print("Did you get a passing grade?", not grade &lt; 60  ) 

# print whether the student got a C using a logical operator

      </code>
      <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("&lt; 80",self.getEditorText(), "Use &lt; 80")
              self.assertIn("&gt;= 70",self.getEditorText(), "Use &gt;= 70")

          myTests().main()
      </tests>
       
      </program>
    </activity>
  
    <activity label="trace-logical-expr">
        <statement>
        <p>Given the code below, what are the values of x that will cause the code to print <c>True</c>?</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code>
print( x &lt; 0 or x &gt;= 10)
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>0 to 10</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>No, because x has to be either negative or 10 or greater for the expression to be true.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>1 to 10</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>No, because x has to be either negative or 10 or greater for the expression to be true.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>negative values</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is partially correct. But what values would be true because they made ``x &gt;=
              10`` true?</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>negative values or 2+ digits</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! x has to be either negative or 10 or greater for the expression to be true. Any number 10 or greater has at least 2 digits.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
      </activity>

      <p>
        In English, we often use an exclusive-or like in the sentence “do you
        want to be player 1 <em>or</em> player 2?” where you can’t be both
        player 1 and player 2. In programming, the or-operator is an
        inclusive-or which means that the whole expression is true if either one
        or the other or <em>both</em> conditions are true.
      </p>

    <note>
      <title>2.2.C.3</title>
      <p>
        The result of a logical expression can be determined by evaluating each condition and then 
        applying not, and, and or to determine whether the overall expression is true or false. 
        Logical expressions can include multiple conditions. Parentheses can be used to group 
        conditions and make the intended order of evaluation clear when combining not, and, and or.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>For complex logical expressions, it helps to first figure out the values of each boolean expression and then
      put it together with the logical operators. For example, try to figure out the logical expression 
      <c>((age &gt;= 12) and (age &lt;= 18)) or (occupation == "student")</c> in the code below.</p>

  <activity label="trace-logical-expr2">
   <statement><p>What is printed when you run this code? Try the CodeLens button to step through the 
    code. Then, change the value of age to 19, try to guess the result, and run to check. 
    Change the value of occupation and run again. </p>
  </statement>       
    <program language="python" interactive="activecode">
      <code>
age = 15
occupation = "student"
print("Eligible for discount?", ((age &gt;= 12) and (age &lt;= 18)) or (occupation == "student") )
# Evaluate this in multiple steps
# Is age &gt;= 12?
greater_than_12 = age &gt;= 12
print("Is age &gt;= 12?", greater_than_12)
# Is age &lt;= 18?
less_than_18 = age &lt;= 18
print("Is age &lt;= 18?", less_than_18)
# Is age between 12 and 18?
age_between_12_and_18 = greater_than_12 and less_than_18
# Is occupation == "student"?
is_student = occupation == "student"
# Put it all together
print("Eligible for discount?", age_between_12_and_18 or is_student)
      </code>
    <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("True",self.getOutput(),"Output")
              self.assertIn("19",self.getEditorText(), "Try changing the value of age to 19")

          myTests().main()
    </tests>
    </program>
  </activity>


  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="unplugged-logic-project">
    <title>Unplugged Logic Project</title>

    <p>Do the following project in a group of 4 people. If you don't have a group, imagine that you are doing this activity
      with 3 other friends or family members and write down their names and ages. (Thank you
            to Jill Westerlund of Hoover High School and Art Lopez of Sweetwater
            High School for this activity suggestion).
    </p>
    <project label="unplugged-venn-diagram">
      <statement>
        <ol>
          <li>Draw or print a <url
            href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1lpjk0LS_KdAddRurMayJZmaFzeyEg4FyhviZcSTXvtU/edit?usp=sharing">Venn
            diagram</url> of 4 intersecting circles. </li>
          <li>Put each of the names of the 4 people in your group in a circle of the Venn Diagram (one name in each circle). </li>
          <li>Write down the age of each person in your group in the circles. If two or more people are the
            same age, put the age in the intersecting parts of their circles. </li>
          <li>
            Write a Boolean expression that compares the age of each person in
            the group using <c>==</c>, <c>&lt;</c>, <c>&gt;</c>, 
            <c>and</c>, for example Ada’s age <c>&gt;</c> Alan’s age
            <c>and</c> Alan’s age <c>==</c> Grace’s age. </li>
          <li> Then, ask each
            person in your group their favorite movie. If two or more people
            have the same favorite movie, put the movie in the intersecting
            parts of their circles. </li>
          <li>Write a Boolean expression that compares the
            favorite movies in the group using <c>==</c>, <c>!=</c>, and
            <c>and</c>, for example Ada’s movie <c>==</c> Alan’s movie
            <c>and</c> Alan’s movie <c>!=</c> Grace’s movie. 
            </li><li>Think of 1
            more comparison and write it in the circles and as a Boolean
            expression. </li>
            <li>Share the Boolean expressions with the class and type them in below. 
              </li>
              </ol>
          </statement>
          <response/>
        </project>
  </subsection>

   <subsection xml:id="LOs-2-2">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>
    <p>After completing this lesson, you should be able to:</p>
    <remark>
      <title>2.2.A</title>
      <p>
        Identify the use of Boolean expressions to control an app's behavior as you interact with the app.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>2.2.B</title>
      <p>
        Develop code using relational operators, and determine the result of relational expressions.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>2.2.C</title>
      <p>
        Develop code using logical operators, and determine the result of logical expressions.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-2-2">
    <title>Vocabulary Review</title>

    <activity label="vocab-boolean">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in this lesson. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Find the words in the lesson above and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
        <premise>Boolean</premise>
        <response>a data type that can only have the values True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>Boolean expression</premise>
          <response>an expression that evaluates to either True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>relational operator</premise>
          <response>an operator like <c>==</c> or <c>&lt;</c> that compares two values and 
            returns True or False</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>logical operator</premise>
          <response>an operator like <c>and</c>, <c>or</c>, or <c>not</c> that combines Boolean 
            expressions and returns True or False</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
